A||N||2||Bill Shenefelt||V 6 4 speed transmission removal||||1||
Z||000000||Bill Shenefelt||12-01-2001||07:20 AM||||I have had trouble with disengagement of the clutch in my 86 v6 4 speed. (worn damaged fork posting a week or so ago) Got great advice on changing of the arm to the fork shaft, but now although the clutch "feels right" I still probably have a worn fork.  I saw that there was information somewhere as to transmission removal thru the wheel well, but I can't seem to find it.  I sort of need a reasonabe step by step removal process to get the tranny out far enough to change the fork shaft and, while I am in there, clutch assy.  (Cradle is not going to come down without a torch job)<BR>Ps Thanks for the great infromation received from the last post I made<BR>Bill Shenefelt<P>------------------<BR>Bill Shenefelt||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-01-2001||08:08 AM||||Unfortunately the cradle must be swung down to get the tranny out regardless of the method used. The rear cradle bolts must be removed to do this procedure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LS1swap||12-01-2001||03:05 PM||||To get my rear cradle bolts off I had to put my jack from my Fiero between the cradle and frame and crank it tight. This puts enough pressure on the nut that it wouldn't spin. If that does not work drill about a 1-1/2" hole aft of the bolt this will allow you to get a torch on it to heat it, and beak it loose. Personally I have always taken the cradle all the way out. It gives you much more room to work.||||reg||1
